<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Lets Crashlytics start without its Gradle plugin.

  The SDK refuses to start without a build ID, and only the Crashlytics Gradle
  plugin writes one. It refuses by throwing from the component's constructor
  (CrashlyticsCore.onPreExecute in firebase-crashlytics 20.0.6), and the
  component is eager in the default FirebaseApp, which FirebaseInitProvider
  creates before any app code runs: the whole process dies on launch, in every
  build, production included, and collection being off makes no difference.

  The build ID only matches an uploaded R8 mapping file to a report. This app
  uploads none (the plugin's upload task would add a network dependency to
  every release build, the tagged one included), and the reports that matter
  here are Dart errors, whose stacks are not obfuscated. So the requirement is
  switched off rather than satisfied.

  The SDK reads the value by name at runtime and nothing else refers to it, so
  the resource shrinker would delete it from a release build — and that build
  would die on launch while every debug build worked. It is kept in
  res/raw/keep.xml: the shrinker honours `tools:keep` only in a raw resource,
  and silently ignores it here.
-->
<resources>
    <bool name="com.crashlytics.RequireBuildId">false</bool>
</resources>
